# Copy to ci_identifier_test.env (ignored by Git). Keep filled files private.
# The repository is PUBLIC. Never commit filled fixtures or full machine reports: even without
# credentials they contain target/record metadata. Store detailed evidence in the target PDI KB.
# One canonical standalone tester; the historical filename is retained for existing downloads.
# Inspect without credentials or writes:
#   python3 test_ci_identifier_precedence.py --list-tests
#   python3 test_ci_identifier_precedence.py --plan
# Ordinary test mode uses approved existing fixtures (Basic or OAuth identity):
#   python3 test_ci_identifier_precedence.py --fixture target-fixtures.json --report-file dti-report.json
# Explicit Basic-admin DEV setup builds the catalog from the baseline settings below plus owned data;
# no externally authored fixture JSON is needed. Review --plan first. Replace DEV host consistently:
#   python3 test_ci_identifier_precedence.py --provision-dev-fixtures --confirm-fixture-target https://dev123456.service-now.com --prepare-fixtures-only --fixture-output dti-reference-fixtures.json --report-file dti-setup-report.json
# Then run setup+the full manifest, allowing a temporary default-CI lease ONLY when needed:
#   python3 test_ci_identifier_precedence.py --provision-dev-fixtures --confirm-fixture-target https://dev123456.service-now.com --allow-default-ci-property --fixture-output dti-reference-fixtures.json --fixture-recovery-file dti-default-ci-recovery.json --report-file dti-report.json
# Use a NEW recovery-journal path for each lease; retain it privately. Recover an interrupted lease:
#   python3 test_ci_identifier_precedence.py --restore-fixture-default-ci dti-default-ci-recovery.json --confirm-fixture-target https://dev123456.service-now.com --report-file dti-recovery-report.json
# Recovery needs connection credentials only, not CI/caller/group fixtures; do not combine it with setup.
# No fixed duration/count is promised: use the plan and actual JSON report.
# Ordinary mode retains all test records; lifecycle PATCH is limited to this run's owned Incidents/Alerts.
# Ordinary mode has no deletes, Scripts - Background, schema changes, or security grants.
# Explicit setup uses an embedded allowlisted Global Background script, requires verified TLS and
# exact HTTPS dev<digits>.service-now.com confirmation, and creates only five Global u_* fields:
# cmdb_ci_business_app.u_car_id string(100); cmdb_ci.u_level_2_support_assignee_group reference
# sys_user_group(32); incident.u_usb_em_test_value string(255); incident.u_netcool_ticket boolean;
# incident.u_generating_alert reference em_alert(32). Existing mismatches abort; no field is retyped.
# Owned CI/service/offering data uses IRE. Marked fixtures/schema persist; no claim table/task column.
# Setup never adds roles, ACLs, cross-scope grants or operational group membership. It requires an
# already valid default-caller property and active unique-name assignee in the configured group.
# Created users are locked-out, web-service-only, role-free and email-blank (not login identities).
# --allow-default-ci-property permits ONLY x_usbna_usb_event.default_cmdb_ci_sys_id around THREE
# non-DTI controls if no valid default already exists. An existing valid default is reused unchanged.
# A private 0600 journal precedes the mutation; finally restores exact value/absence and verifies
# DB/cache. Only an exact setup-created property row may be removed to restore original absence.
# Recovery refuses concurrent external changes; any restoration failure is ERROR/nonzero.
# This shared property briefly affects unrelated unhinted events: use an approved DEV window.
# Generated catalogs are private 0600 files, target-bound, and not portable PDI-to-production assets.
# Prefer an existing ignored private directory such as .local-evidence/ for ALL generated
# catalogs, journals and machine reports; arbitrary output filenames are not automatically ignored.
# A prepared catalog alone cannot prove positive default-CI coverage when the property is absent;
# the temporary lease requires the explicit administrative setup/full-run flags above.
# No mode installs a Linux service, root process, scheduler, monitoring email or claim table.
# A live run creates dozens of retained records and executes normal platform BRs/notifications;
# native workflow/mail is NOT suppressed. Review the test caller, group, and identity first.
SN_INSTANCE_URL=https://your-instance.service-now.com

# Choose Basic OR OAuth client credentials. Base64 is reversible encoding, not encryption.
# Plain SN_USERNAME / SN_PASSWORD are also supported. Prefer b64 for whitespace-containing passwords.
SN_USERNAME_B64=
SN_PASSWORD_B64=
USBEM_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
USBEM_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
USBEM_OAUTH_SCOPE=
# USBEM_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID_B64 and USBEM_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET_B64 are also supported.
# Existing verifier production profiles work with --env-file usbem_verify.env --profile itsmnowDEVworker
# using USBEM_ITSMNOWDEVWORKER_URL / OAUTH_CLIENT_ID / OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET / OAUTH_SCOPE.
# Add this template's fixture keys to that file. Per-profile USBEM_<PROFILE>_<KEY> overrides work.
# A selected profile's URL MUST be non-empty: otherwise stop before network, no global-PDI fallback.
# Explicit --instance wins. Unselected blank profiles are ignored (DEV-first setup is supported).
# A profile name that normalizes to an empty key is a configuration error.

USBEM_SOURCE=genericJsonV2
USBEM_EXPECTED_RELEASE=2026.10.29.3
# The tester has its own suite version (1.2.0), separate from the runtime release.
# The plan/report also records the executing script's SHA-256 captured at module load.

# Instance-specific fixtures: two real, DIFFERENT CIs. Configure expectations from trusted CMDB data.
# Name-only and identifier-only control incidents prove the fixtures before conflict cases run.
# Identifier must uniquely select EXPECTED_CI_SYS_ID; decoy name must select DECOY_CI_SYS_ID.
USBEM_CI_TYPE=cmdb_ci_server
USBEM_CI_IDENTIFIER_JSON={"serial_number":"replace-with-unique-serial"}
USBEM_EXPECTED_CI_SYS_ID=replace-with-identifier-selected-ci-sys-id
USBEM_DECOY_CI_NAME=replace-with-different-existing-ci-name
USBEM_DECOY_CI_SYS_ID=replace-with-decoy-ci-sys-id
# Complete coverage requires a non-empty group fixture and exact expected group sys_id.
USBEM_ASSIGNMENT_GROUP=
# If assignment group is a sys_id it is verified automatically. When using a name, supply its sys_id.
USBEM_EXPECTED_ASSIGNMENT_GROUP_SYS_ID=

# Required incident field checks. Use a valid test caller from THIS instance.
# Category/subcategory input can be a choice label or stored value; expected values are stored API values.
# Configure choices that exist in each instance before running the test.
USBEM_TEST_CALLER_SYS_ID=replace-with-existing-test-caller-sys-id
USBEM_TEST_CATEGORY=software
USBEM_EXPECTED_CATEGORY=software
USBEM_TEST_SUBCATEGORY=os
USBEM_EXPECTED_SUBCATEGORY=os
USBEM_TEST_IMPACT=2
USBEM_TEST_URGENCY=2

# Ordinary full testing requires the reference-fixture JSON catalog described in the KB.
# Use --fixture /path/target-fixtures.json, or place the JSON object on one line in this variable.
# Explicit DEV setup can generate/top up this catalog from baseline settings and marked owned data;
# leave this variable blank when no externally authored catalog is needed for that setup.
# A missing fixture, required read permission, or unperformed check makes the report INCOMPLETE,
# never a passing release sign-off. Do not copy PDI sys_ids to production.
# JSON top-level keys: caller_name, assignee_sys_id, assignee_name, group_name,
# default_caller_sys_id, ci_support_sys_id, ci_support_group_sys_id, service_sys_id,
# service_name, offering_sys_id, offering_name, default_category, default_subcategory,
# and advanced_cases. The last two are independently verified stored values for the runtime's
# Software / Monitoring Alert defaults, not the override choices configured above.
# Do not derive default_category solely from sys_choice label metadata. DEV's parity default
# stores literal Software (not canonical choice software); required fields filled != valid taxonomy.
# Prefer explicit stored choice values in production. Preserve raw failures when correcting fixtures.
# Optional target settings: severity_map (each severity -> [impact, urgency]), terminal_close_code,
# alert_closed_state, correlation_length, duplicate_work_note_enabled (JSON boolean, default true).
# duplicate_work_note_enabled must match the actual target property; the tester does not change it.
# If local Incident resolution requires a cause, JSON terminal_extra_fields may contain ONLY
# a non-empty string u_cause, e.g. {"u_cause":"Testing DTI"}. The field must already exist;
# its stored value is read back after the owned Incident update. No other extra fields are writable.
# The foreign-task guard additionally needs foreign_task_sys_id and optional foreign_task_marker
# (default "[TEST] USBEM"). Preprovision a marked non-Incident Task whose short description
# STARTS with that marker. The marker must itself start "[TEST] USBEM"; never supply a real work task.
# It reads that task and links only its own Alert; it never writes the preprovisioned Task.
# Explicit DEV setup may create a marked base Task. On DEV the supported Task reference is
# em_alert.incident -> task (polymorphic), NOT a nonexistent em_alert.task column.
# Advanced cases: {payload:{overrides}, remove_fields:[inherited keys to remove],
# expected_incident:{stored fields}, expected_event:{stored fields},
# expected_additional_info:{exact JSON values}, absent_additional_info:[keys],
# want_incident:true/false, want_alert:true/false}. Empty expected strings assert blank fields.
# User additional_info retains native JSON booleans/numbers/arrays/objects. Do not stringify them.
# Connector-added routing references and DTI flags/values use their operational string contract.
# Root Event/SPECIAL aliases (including envelope dti_impact) beat conflicting wrapped aliases;
# per-record real Incident fields such as impact/category/caller_id overlay envelope passthrough.
# A feature value may be an array of case objects. offering_parent_filter needs positive and
# negative parent controls; default_dummy_policy needs THREE distinct controls: a positive valid
# configured default CI, invalid-explicit-CI suppression of that default, and no-hint blank CI.
# Without a real non-empty configured default CI, ordinary mode leaves the family INCOMPLETE.
# Only explicit DEV setup + --allow-default-ci-property can temporarily supply and restore it.
# Canonical names must differ beyond letter case.
# Other advanced Incident-field families require want_incident:true (a JSON boolean);
# disabling Incident creation cannot be used to skip their stored-field assertions.
# All advanced feature names and a partial catalog example are in the KB; --list-tests shows names.
USBEM_TEST_FIXTURES_JSON=

# Each POST is attempted ONCE under the separate fast-return budget below, never blindly retried.
# Stored observations use bounded monotonic deadlines. This template supplies the vetted full-
# manifest settings: 240s observation budget, 30s continuous stability, 10s fast POST budget.
# Raw CLI fallback 120s remains for compatibility; it was not live-certified for the full matrix.
# Increase observation headroom for slower Event Management/reconciliation and serial read scans.
USBEM_TEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=240
# Fast DTI must return a valid incident sys_id within this round-trip budget (including auth).
# This caps each POST request; observation continues separately without holding the POST open.
USBEM_FAST_POST_MAX_SECONDS=10
USBEM_TEST_STABLE_SECONDS=30
USBEM_TEST_POLL_SECONDS=3
USBEM_TEST_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=90
# Three simultaneous first events always run. The known duplicate race is reported explicitly;
# COMPLETE_WITH_KNOWN_LIMITATIONS is not a concurrency-safety claim.

# TLS verification is on. Supply the work laptop's PEM corporate trust bundle if required.
# CLI --ca-bundle overrides this value. --insecure is an explicit last resort.
USBEM_CA_BUNDLE=

# Required evidence access: connector POST; Event/Incident/Alert reads including Event state,
# Incident required fields/correlation/references, and Alert state/task/Incident/additional_info;
# sys_journal_field reads for exact journal occurrence counts; sys_attachment metadata and file reads.
# Fixture validation also reads cmdb_ci, sys_user_group, cmdb_ci_service, service_offering,
# and the named default-CI sys_properties record. Missing metadata/fixture reads make coverage incomplete.
# The ambiguous-user control also reads sys_user exact-name matches to prove actual ambiguity.
# foreign_task_guard reads its marked Task baseline and needs the actual Alert Task-reference
# field's read/write access (em_alert.incident on DEV). Ordinary mode needs no Task write/create.
# Missing fixtures/permissions block that guard; no em_alert.task column is added.
# The lifecycle/ownership scenarios update only owned test Incidents and Alerts through Table API.
# Missing/hidden fields, denied reads, or denied lifecycle writes block complete sign-off.
# Default cases omit wait-for-incident keys. Legacy waiting is not certified by the default run.
# Production-specific rules and identities must be exercised on each selected target.
# Runtime CI identifier lookup uses cmdb_ci + INSTANCEOF; resource lookup may directly query
# the configured CI child class (for example cmdb_ci_server). Its separate scoped read grant
# must be reviewed per target; identifier success does not prove the resource path is allowed.
# DEV's exact cmdb_ci_server/read request was approved and read back allowed; that grant is
# target-specific and is not permission to copy the privilege record or grant other tables.
